-- Scene 1: the loop states its own coverage, at RUN TIME --
  [OK] the loop answers with a coverage statement
  [OK] ...it says what it SCHEDULES
  [OK] ...and, which matters more, what it CANNOT SEE
  [OK] ...naming the clock it does not read
  [OK] ...and the event bus it cannot look at, because that bus is in ANOTHER DLL
  [OK] ...and it does not pretend to judge whether a coverage statement is TRUE

-- Scene 2: registration is a GATE, not a formality --
  [OK] an agent with NO coverage statement is refused
  [OK] ...with a NAMED code, the way a Ring C-error is named
  [OK] ...naming the agent it is about
  [OK] ...and citing the law rather than just complaining
  [OK] ...and nothing was registered
  [OK] an agent with NO reversibility class is refused too
  [OK] ...and the refusal says which words are acceptable
  [OK] an agent that declares BOTH registers
  [OK] ...and the loop hands its coverage statement back
  [OK] ...and its reversibility class
  [OK] the same slot cannot register twice

-- Scene 3: ONE RULE, TWO LAYERS, SAME WORDS --
  [OK] an llm actor holding 'effectful' is REFUSED at registration
  [OK] ...in the graph rule's own sentence
  [OK] an llm actor WITHOUT it is a slot like any other
  [OK] the Ring layer still carries the same rule, by that name
  [OK] ...and the engine quotes ITS violation message, not a rewrite of it

-- Scene 4: priority orders the pass; registration breaks the tie --
  [OK] one pass makes all four due
  [OK] the highest priority runs first
  [OK] equal priorities run in REGISTRATION order
  [OK] and the lowest runs last

-- Scene 5: the same sequence gives the same order, twice --
  [OK] the script really did visit things
  [OK] both runs visited the same NUMBER of times
  [OK] ...and in the same ORDER, agent for agent

-- Scene 6: mailboxes are DRAINED, and they are a real queue --
  [OK] an empty inbox schedules nothing
  [OK] three sends leave THREE messages, not one
  [OK] a non-empty inbox schedules the agent
  [OK] ...and the reason given is 'mailbox', not 'timer'
  [OK] the queue is FIFO
  [OK] ...still FIFO

-- Scene 7: a remade channel does not make an agent go DEAF --
  [OK] a new generation resyncs the baseline instead of ignoring the events
  [OK] ...so the three fresh events are three visits

-- Scene 8: stzAgentHost adopts the loop, API unchanged --
  [OK] a fresh host runs the RING pump, exactly as it always did
  [OK] adopting the loop with UNDECLARED agents is REFUSED
  [OK] ...and the host carries the ENGINE's diagnostic, not its own paraphrase
  [OK] declared agents are adopted
  [OK] ...each landing in an engine slot
  [OK] the SAME RunFor() call now pumps from Zig
  [OK] ...and both agents ran
  [OK] the trace records the loop's REASON for each visit
  [OK] ...and alpha, the higher priority, was visited first in the pass

-- Scene 9: cancel, resume and retire reach INTO the loop --
  [OK] a cancelled agent stops being SCHEDULED, not merely skipped
  [OK] ...while the other agent keeps running
  [OK] resume puts it back in the schedule
  [OK] retirement without governance is granted
  [OK] ...and the engine slot is released

-- Scene 10: an over-full queue DROPS, and SAYS SO --
  [OK] the loop decided on all 300 visits
  [OK] ...but the queue holds its bound
  [OK] ...and the 44 it could not hold are COUNTED, never silently lost

